  "paper_core_claims": [
    {
      "claim": "DeepONet represents a nonlinear operator through a branch network over finitely sampled input-function values and a trunk network over output coordinates, combined by an inner product plus optional bias.",
      "anchors": "Fig. 1 and pp. 219-220"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Under compact-domain, compact input-family, continuity, and universal-approximator assumptions, the generalized branch/trunk architecture can approximate continuous nonlinear operators uniformly.",
      "anchors": "Theorems 1-2 and Supplement [AXIS_CODE]-[AXIS_CODE]"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The theorem controls representation or approximation capacity, not optimization error, finite-sample generalization, network-size complexity, or learnability; these remain separate and often dominant.",
      "anchors": "pp. 220-221 and conclusion p. 227"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Across 16 curated explicit, implicit, deterministic, and stochastic examples generated primarily from known numerical solvers, the authors report lower generalization error than selected baselines and fast empirical error decay in several regimes.",
      "anchors": "Figs. 2-6 and Supplement S7-S17"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The paper's stochastic examples propagate random-process inputs or predict pathwise/statistical outputs, but they do not establish a unified calibrated uncertainty interface for unseen production distributions.",
      "anchors": "pp. 225-227 and Supplement [AXIS_CODE], S7"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Because the training targets are produced by reference solvers, the trained surrogate cannot exceed those solvers' accuracy on that evidence; the paper claims inference speedup after expensive offline training, not autonomous correctness.",
      "anchors": "conclusion p. 227 and Supplement Tables [AXIS_CODE]-S5"
    }
  ],
  "function_concept_used_by_paper": "A function is an element of a compact family in C(K), operationalized by values at shared sensor points, orthogonal-polynomial coefficients, an image grid, or a truncated Karhunen-Loève representation. An operator maps an input function u to an output function G(u); the trained artifact evaluates G(u)(y) for a queried output coordinate y.",
  "representation_or_carrier": {
    "mathematical_carrier": "compact subsets of Banach spaces of continuous functions and continuous nonlinear operators",
    "input_carrier": "shared sensor vector [u(x1),...,u(xm)], polynomial expansion coefficients, image/grid values, or KL modes",
    "output_carrier": "coordinate y and scalar G(u)(y), repeated over arbitrary output locations to reconstruct an output function",
    "artifact_carrier": "branch/trunk network parameters, architecture and hyperparameters, generated train/test triples, solver outputs, and saved model files in the linked implementation"
  },
  "specification_language": "The paper specifies operators through mathematical domains, equations, boundary or initial conditions, input-function distributions, sensor sets, output coordinates, and accuracy norms. These form a strong research-level FunctionSpec for each experiment, but there is no executable cross-domain schema for units, tensor shapes, permissions, side effects, lifecycle policy, or semantic compatibility.",
  "compiler_or_generator": {
    "data_generation": "Known ODE/PDE solvers, analytic solutions, Gaussian random fields, orthogonal-polynomial samples, and KL expansions generate supervised triples (u,y,G(u)(y)).",
    "artifact_generation": "Optimization fits branch and trunk subnetworks so their latent-vector inner product approximates G(u)(y).",
    "theoretical_generator": "Theorems 1-2 motivate representational form; Theorem 3 bounds sensor discretization for a restricted class of Lipschitz dynamical systems.",
    "boundary": "No general algorithm derives data, architecture, sample size, or a certified artifact from an arbitrary operator specification. Hyperparameters are manually tuned and theorem assumptions must be established separately."
  },
  "interpreter_or_runtime": "At inference, the branch network embeds a fixed-sensor representation of u, the trunk network embeds y, and their dot product plus bias returns an approximation of G(u)(y). TensorFlow/DeepXDE, numerical precision, preprocessing, sensor alignment, and dependencies are part of the concrete runtime but not captured by the mathematical theorem.",
  "composition_rule": "Within one artifact, branch and trunk embeddings compose by a finite inner product, and output coordinates can include physical, parameter, or stochastic dimensions. The work discusses specialized subnetworks and prospective multiphysics extensions, but it does not define typed composition among independently trained operators or runtime routing, fallback, and conflict resolution.",
  "state_or_effect_model": "The mathematical operator is treated as a pure mapping, while the training workflow has mutable weights, random sampling and initialization, solver/file I/O, accelerator computation, and checkpoints. These operational effects are described procedurally, not governed by a typed effect, permission, transaction, or rollback model.",
  "uncertainty_model": "Reported experimental uncertainty is mainly mean and one standard deviation over repeated runs, and stochastic examples use Gaussian-process/KL inputs and pathwise or mean/standard-deviation targets. The paper also relates one GRF distribution shift to a 2-Wasserstein distance. It does not provide calibrated predictive intervals, epistemic/aleatoric separation, coverage guarantees, or end-to-end uncertainty propagation for arbitrary deployments; one nonlinear-ODE analysis removes the worst 0.1% predictions.",
  "validation_regime": {
    "theoretical": "uniform approximation theorems on compact continuous-operator settings, a sensor-count result for restricted Lipschitz systems, and Hölder-continuity arguments for studied operators",
    "empirical": "16 generated problems spanning explicit transforms, deterministic ODE/PDE solution operators, and stochastic ODE/PDE mappings",
