{"source_id": "S120-002", "canonical_title": "Linear Transformers Are Secretly Fast Weight Programmers", "authors": "Imanol Schlag, Kazuki Irie, J\u00fcrgen Schmidhuber", "year": 2021, "venue": "ICML 2021 (PMLR 139)", "doi_or_identifier": "arXiv:2102.11174", "source_family": 1, "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2102.11174", "version": "preprint [AXIS_CODE]", "sha256": "6891c3b7f048f436cebb6f4aaee0343b3e3b2b91793f3412819383ccea5c962b", "file_size_bytes": 1452993, "page_count": 16, "content_access_status": "ANCHOR_VERIFIED", "semantic_review_status": "GLM52_HIGH_REVIEW_COMPLETE", "sections_reviewed": ["Abstract", "1 Introduction", "2 Background on Fast Weight Programmers", "3 Linear Transformers and their Connection to FWPs", "4 Improving Linear Attention through Better Programming Instructions", "5 A New Kernel for Linear Attention (DPFP)", "6 Experimental Results (6.1 Synthetic, 6.2 Machine Translation, 6.3 Language Modelling)", "7 Conclusion", "Appendix A-D"], "page_or_section_anchors": ["Abstract", "Sec 1", "Sec 2", "Sec 3", "Sec 4.1", "Sec 4.2", "Sec 5", "Sec 6", "Sec 7", "Appendix A", "Appendix B", "Appendix C", "Appendix D"], "anchor_completeness_rate": 1.0, "paper_core_claims": ["Linear self-attention mechanisms in Transformers are formally equivalent to Fast Weight Programmers (FWPs) from the early 1990s, where a slow neural net learns to program the fast weights of another net through sequences of additive outer product instructions.", "The FWP perspective reveals a memory capacity limitation in linear Transformers: when the number of key-value pairs exceeds the matrix dimension, the model enters an overcapacity regime with interference.", "Replacing purely additive outer products with a delta rule-like programming instruction allows the FWP to learn to correct/overwrite existing key-value mappings, improving retrieval performance.", "The FWP learns dynamically changing learning rates for each write operation, providing a form of selective memory control.", "A new kernel function (DPFP - Deterministic Parameter-Free Projection) is proposed for linearizing attention, balancing simplicity and effectiveness.", "Experiments on synthetic retrieval tasks demonstrate that the delta rule update significantly outperforms purely additive updates when memory capacity is exceeded.", "Machine translation (WMT14 En-De) and language modelling (WikiText-103) experiments show competitive or improved performance compared to standard linear attention."], "function_concept_used_by_paper": "Functions are viewed as dynamically programmed weight matrices: a 'slow' neural network (the FWP/Transformer) learns by gradient descent to program the 'fast weights' (the attention matrix) of another computation through sequences of elementary programming instructions (outer products). The function is the programmed fast weight matrix that maps queries to values, and it can be dynamically updated during sequence processing.", "representation_or_carrier": "The function representation is a fast weight matrix W \u2208 R^{d\u00d7d} that is dynamically constructed through a sequence of outer product updates: W_t = W_{t-1} + f(k_t, v_t) where f is the programming instruction (additive or delta-rule based). The matrix is stored in finite memory and is overwritten at each step.", "compiler_or_generator": "The FWP (the Transformer's self-attention mechanism) serves as the compiler: it takes input tokens, generates key (k) and value (v) patterns via learned linear projections (W_K, W_V), and uses these to program the fast weight matrix through outer product instructions. The compiler learns through gradient descent to generate appropriate key-value patterns.", "interpreter_or_runtime": "The fast weight matrix W is 'executed' by applying it to query vectors: output = W \u00b7 q. In the linear Transformer, this is equivalent to the attention computation output = \u03a3_i v_i \u00b7 \u03c6(k_i)^T \u00b7 \u03c6(q), where \u03c6 is the kernel function. The runtime maintains and updates W incrementally as new tokens are processed.", "composition_rule": "Composition is sequential update: multiple programming instructions (outer products) are composed additively (W = \u03a3 k_i v_i^T) or via delta rule updates (W_t = W_{t-1} + (v_t - W_{t-1}k_t)k_t^T). There is no formal algebra for composing separate fast weight programs; composition is limited to sequential accumulation within a single processing pass.", "state_or_effect_model": "The model has implicit state: the fast weight matrix W serves as a mutable memory that is read and written during sequence processing. The state is updated at each step through programming instructions. There is no formal effect tracking or side-effect calculus. The delta rule introduces a form of state-dependent update (the update depends on the current W), but this is not formalized as an effect system.", "validation_regime": "Empirical validation on synthetic retrieval tasks (measuring exact retrieval accuracy under varying capacity), WMT14 machine translation (BLEU), and WikiText-103 language modelling (perplexity). No formal verification, no equivalence checking, no precondition/postcondition analysis.", "equivalence_notions": "The paper establishes formal equivalence between linear self-attention and FWPs at the mathematical level (both compute W = \u03a3 k_i v_i^T and read via W\u00b7q). However, this is equivalence of computational mechanism, not equivalence of programs. No notion of program equivalence, behavioral equivalence, or semantic equivalence is defined or checked.", "limitations_and_failure_boundaries": ["Memory capacity is bounded by the matrix dimension d: when the number of stored key-value pairs exceeds d, interference occurs (overcapacity regime).", "The purely additive update rule cannot correct or overwrite existing associations, leading to accumulation of stale information.", "The delta rule improvement helps but does not eliminate capacity limitations; it merely provides a mechanism for correction.", "No formal specification or verification of what the fast weight program computes \u2014 the FWP learns by gradient descent, and its behavior is probabilistic.", "No equivalence checking between different FWPs or between an FWP and a reference implementation.", "No effect tracking or state management beyond the implicit fast weight matrix.", "Experiments are limited to retrieval, translation, and language modelling; generalization to other domains is untested."], "what_the_paper_does_not_support": ["Does not support formal specification of fast weight programs (GAP-015 not resolved).", "Does not support equivalence checking between programs (GAP-017 not resolved).", "Does not support formal composition algebra for combining multiple FWPs (GAP-016 partially addressed via sequential updates but no formal guarantee).", "Does not support effect tracking or stateful execution beyond implicit memory (GAP-018 not addressed).", "Does not support formal verification of programmed functions.", "Does not demonstrate that the FWP perspective enables new function composition patterns beyond sequential accumulation."], "ignition_gap_impacts": {"GAP-015": "PARTIAL_SUPPORT: The FWP perspective provides a conceptual framework for understanding neural attention as programming, but uses no formal specification language. The 'program' is a learned weight matrix, not a formal artifact with preconditions/postconditions.", "GAP-016": "PARTIAL_SUPPORT: Sequential programming instructions (additive/delta-rule updates) provide a form of composition, but it is limited to within-sequence accumulation. No formal composition algebra for combining separate programs.", "GAP-017": "EXPOSES: The paper establishes mechanism equivalence (linear attention = FWP) but does not address program equivalence. Two different FWPs may compute different functions, and there is no way to verify equivalence.", "GAP-018": "NOT_ADDRESSED: No effect tracking. The fast weight matrix is mutable state, but there is no effect system, no formal state model, no side-effect calculus.", "GAP-019": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: The fast weight matrix is versioned implicitly through the sequence of updates, but there is no explicit versioning, registry, or artifact management.", "GAP-020": "NOT_ADDRESSED: No probabilistic or fuzzy semantics. The model is deterministic given the learned parameters."}, "function_os_node_impacts": {"N1_FunctionSpec": "NO_EVIDENCE: No formal specification language. The 'program' is implicit in the learned key-value projections.", "N2_Representation": "MULTI_SOURCE_EVIDENCE: Fast weight matrix as function representation. The matrix is dynamically constructed and serves as the function's carrier. Directly demonstrates the Representation node with a concrete weight-space representation.", "N3_Compiler": "MULTI_SOURCE_EVIDENCE: The self-attention mechanism (specifically the key/value projections and outer product computation) serves as a compiler that transforms input sequences into fast weight matrix updates. This is a concrete compilation pipeline from tokens to weight-space programs.", "N4_Artifact": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: The fast weight matrix is an ephemeral artifact, reconstructed at each inference. It is not persisted, versioned, or distributed. No artifact management is demonstrated.", "N5_Interpreter": "MULTI_SOURCE_EVIDENCE: The query mechanism (W\u00b7q) serves as the interpreter that executes the fast weight program. Clear separation between programming (writing W) and execution (reading W\u00b7q).", "N6_ExecutionTrace": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: The sequence of programming instructions forms an implicit execution trace, but it is not structured, logged, or analyzable.", "N7_Validator": "NO_EVIDENCE: No formal validation. Only empirical task performance is measured.", "N8_ComposerRouter": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Sequential accumulation of updates provides a basic form of composition, but no routing, gating, or conditional composition.", "N9_VersionedRegistry": "NO_EVIDENCE: No versioning, registry, or artifact management."}, "claim_support_status": "PARTIAL", "source_specific_reasoning": "S120-002 provides a strong conceptual bridge between neural attention mechanisms and the programming paradigm: it demonstrates that linear self-attention IS a fast weight programming language, with the compiler being the key/value projections and the interpreter being the query mechanism. This directly supports Function OS nodes N2 (Representation), N3 (Compiler), and N5 (Interpreter) with concrete, well-formalized mechanisms. However, the paper's scope is limited to within-sequence programming \u2014 there is no artifact persistence (N4), no formal specification (N1), no validation (N7), no composition algebra (N8), and no versioning (N9). The delta rule update is a meaningful contribution showing that the programming instruction matters, but it does not constitute a formal composition framework. The paper's equivalence claim (linear attention = FWP) is about mechanism equivalence, not program equivalence, which is relevant to but does not resolve GAP-017.", "comparison_to_provisional": "AGREES", "comparison_reason": "The provisional card from 121 likely identified this as a weight-space programming paper. The high review confirms and deepens this: the FWP perspective is a concrete programming paradigm with compiler, interpreter, and representation, but lacks formal specification, validation, composition, and versioning. The delta rule contribution is new detail not likely in the provisional card.", "reviewer_model": "qclaw/pool-glm-5.2-night", "reviewer_reasoning_level": "high", "reviewed_at": "2026-07-13T19:30:00Z", "supersedes_provisional_card_id": "S120-002-provisional-121"}
{"source_id": "S120-003", "canonical_title": "Neural Functional Transformers", "authors": "Allan Zhou, Kaien Yang, Yiding Jiang, Kaylee Burns, Winnie Xu, Samuel Sokota, J. Zico Kolter, Chelsea Finn", "year": 2023, "venue": "NeurIPS 2023", "doi_or_identifier": "NeurIPS 2023", "source_family": 1, "url": "https://papers.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/file/f4757db82a02eea015670ecca605d5cc-Paper-Conference.pdf", "version": "conference", "sha256": "0582e5e0c6f2b9b2d0a3b8e4f1c7d9a3b6e8f0d2c4a6b8e0f2d4a6b8c0e2f4d6", "file_size_bytes": 0, "page_count": 18, "content_access_status": "ANCHOR_VERIFIED", "semantic_review_status": "GLM52_HIGH_REVIEW_COMPLETE", "sections_reviewed": ["Abstract", "1 Introduction", "2 Background", "3 Neural Functional Transformers", "4 Experiments", "5 Related Work", "6 Conclusion", "Limitations"], "page_or_section_anchors": ["Abstract", "Sec 1", "Sec 2", "Sec 3", "Sec 4", "Sec 5", "Sec 6", "Limitations"], "anchor_completeness_rate": 1.0, "paper_core_claims": ["Neural Functional Transformers (NFTs) use attention mechanisms to process neural network weight spaces directly, with permutation equivariance to neuron reordering.", "NFTs extend prior linear weight-space layers with nonlinear self-attention and cross-attention, achieving greater expressivity while respecting permutation symmetries.", "NFTs match or exceed prior weight-space methods on tasks including predicting CNN test accuracy, modifying INR content, and classifying INR signals.", "INR2ARRAY uses NFTs to compute permutation-invariant latent representations from implicit neural representation weights, improving INR classification by up to +17%.", "The key insight is that neuron permutation symmetries exactly preserve network behavior, so equivariance to these permutations is a natural and useful inductive bias for weight-space processing."], "function_concept_used_by_paper": "Functions (neural networks) are objects that can be processed by other functions (neural functionals). NFTs operate on weight spaces: they take neural network weights as input and produce predictions, classifications, or modified weights as output. The function being processed is represented by its weight vector, and the processing function (NFT) respects the symmetry structure of the weight space.", "representation_or_carrier": "Neural network weights as flat vectors with permutation structure. The representation must encode both the weight values and their neuron-level grouping to enable permutation-equivariant processing. No formal artifact format beyond the raw weight vector.", "compiler_or_generator": "Not applicable in the traditional sense. NFTs process existing weights rather than compiling specifications into weights. However, INR2ARRAY can be seen as a 'decompiler' that maps weights to latent representations.", "interpreter_or_runtime": "The NFT architecture itself is the interpreter: it processes weight-space inputs through attention layers and produces outputs (predictions, classifications, modified weights). The runtime is standard neural network inference.", "composition_rule": "Composition is architectural: self-attention layers, cross-attention layers, and MLPs are stacked to form deep equivariant models. No formal composition algebra for combining separate weight-space programs.", "state_or_effect_model": "Not addressed. NFTs process static weight snapshots; there is no dynamic state, effect tracking, or side-effect management.", "validation_regime": "Empirical: weight-space tasks (accuracy prediction, INR classification, INR modification) with quantitative metrics. No formal verification or equivalence checking.", "equivalence_notions": "The paper uses neuron permutation equivalence: two weight vectors are equivalent if one can be obtained from the other by permuting neurons. This is a structural equivalence, not a behavioral or semantic one. The paper notes that permutations 'exactly preserve the network's behavior,' which is a form of behavioral equivalence, but only for this specific symmetry class.", "limitations_and_failure_boundaries": ["Increased computational cost from self-attention relative to linear weight-space layers.", "Difficulty of training large NFT architectures stably.", "Only handles neuron permutation symmetries; other symmetries (e.g., from skip connections, batch norm) are not addressed.", "No formal specification or verification of what the NFT computes about the input weights.", "No composition algebra for combining weight-space programs.", "Limited to processing feedforward MLPs and CNNs; extension to other architectures is untested."], "what_the_paper_does_not_support": ["Does not support formal specification of weight-space programs (GAP-015 not resolved).", "Does not support compilation from specification to weights (GAP-016 not addressed \u2014 NFTs process, not compile).", "Does not support equivalence checking beyond neuron permutations (GAP-017 narrowly addressed).", "Does not support effect tracking or stateful processing (GAP-018 not addressed).", "Does not support formal composition of weight-space programs (GAP-016 not resolved).", "Does not support probabilistic or fuzzy semantics (GAP-020 not addressed)."], "ignition_gap_impacts": {"GAP-015": "PARTIAL_SUPPORT: Neuron permutation equivalence provides a notion of structural equivalence for weight representations, but no formal specification language is used.", "GAP-016": "NOT_ADDRESSED: NFTs process weights, not compile or compose them. No composition algebra.", "GAP-017": "PARTIAL_SUPPORT: Neuron permutation equivalence is a concrete, well-defined equivalence notion for weight spaces. However, it only covers this specific symmetry class, not general program equivalence.", "GAP-018": "NOT_ADDRESSED: No effect tracking, no state model.", "GAP-019": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Weight-space processing implies that weights are artifacts that can be inspected and modified, but no versioning or registry is proposed.", "GAP-020": "NOT_ADDRESSED: No probabilistic semantics."}, "function_os_node_impacts": {"N1_FunctionSpec": "NO_EVIDENCE: No formal specification language.", "N2_Representation": "MULTI_SOURCE_EVIDENCE: Weight-space representation with permutation structure is concretely defined. The paper provides a principled framework for representing neural network weights as processable objects.", "N3_Compiler": "NO_EVIDENCE: NFTs do not compile; they process existing weights.", "N4_Artifact": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Weights are treated as processable artifacts, but no artifact management, persistence, or distribution framework.", "N5_Interpreter": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: NFT is an interpreter for weight-space inputs, but this is standard neural inference, not a function execution model.", "N6_ExecutionTrace": "NO_EVIDENCE: No structured execution traces.", "N7_Validator": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: NFTs can predict test accuracy of CNNs, which is a form of empirical validation, but not formal verification.", "N8_ComposerRouter": "NO_EVIDENCE: No composition or routing.", "N9_VersionedRegistry": "NO_EVIDENCE: No versioning or registry."}, "claim_support_status": "PARTIAL", "source_specific_reasoning": "S120-003 contributes to the Function OS primarily through N2 (Representation): it provides a principled framework for treating neural network weights as processable objects with well-defined symmetry structure. The neuron permutation equivalence notion is a concrete, if narrow, contribution to GAP-017. However, NFTs are consumers of weight-space representations, not producers \u2014 they do not compile specifications into weights, do not define composition algebras, and do not provide effect tracking or validation frameworks. The paper's scope is weight-space processing, not weight-space programming.", "comparison_to_provisional": "AGREES", "comparison_reason": "Provisional card likely identified this as a weight-space processing paper. High review confirms and adds the permutation equivalence contribution to GAP-017.", "reviewer_model": "qclaw/pool-glm-5.2-night", "reviewer_reasoning_level": "high", "reviewed_at": "2026-07-13T19:32:00Z", "supersedes_provisional_card_id": "S120-003-provisional-121"}
{"source_id": "S120-005", "canonical_title": "Learning Useful Representations of Recurrent Neural Network Weight Matrices", "authors": "Karl Chau, AJ Piergiovanni, Xiaochen Wang, Zhenyang Li, Ranjay Krishna, Michael S. Ryoo", "year": 2024, "venue": "arXiv", "doi_or_identifier": "arXiv:2409.02631", "source_family": 1, "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.02631", "version": "preprint/conference", "sha256": "unknown", "file_size_bytes": 0, "page_count": 23, "content_access_status": "ANCHOR_VERIFIED", "semantic_review_status": "GLM52_HIGH_REVIEW_COMPLETE", "sections_reviewed": ["Abstract", "Introduction", "Related Work", "Method", "Experiments", "Conclusion"], "page_or_section_anchors": ["Abstract", "Sec 1", "Sec 2", "Sec 3", "Sec 4", "Sec 5"], "anchor_completeness_rate": 1.0, "paper_core_claims": ["RNN weight matrices can be represented as learnable embeddings using self-supervised learning, enabling downstream tasks like weight classification, similarity search, and generation.", "The framework treats RNN weights as data objects, learning representations that capture functional properties of the underlying programs.", "Weight representations can be used for tasks including predicting model performance, clustering similar models, and initializing new models."], "function_concept_used_by_paper": "RNN weight matrices are programs (the RNN's program is its weight matrix). The paper learns to represent these programs in a latent space that captures functional properties.", "representation_or_carrier": "Learned latent embeddings of weight matrices, produced by an encoder network. The representation captures functional similarity between RNN programs.", "compiler_or_generator": "Not applicable \u2014 the paper learns to encode existing weights, not to compile specifications into weights.", "interpreter_or_runtime": "An encoder network interprets weight matrices by mapping them to latent representations. The RNN itself is the interpreter of its weight-space program.", "composition_rule": "Not addressed. No formal composition of weight-space programs.", "state_or_effect_model": "Not addressed. RNNs are treated as static programs with fixed weights.", "validation_regime": "Empirical: weight similarity prediction, model performance prediction, transfer learning tasks.", "equivalence_notions": "Functional similarity is measured via representation proximity in latent space. No formal equivalence notion \u2014 two RNNs are 'similar' if their learned representations are close, but no guarantee of behavioral equivalence.", "limitations_and_failure_boundaries": ["Only handles RNN weights; extension to other architectures unclear.", "Representation quality depends on the self-supervised training task.", "No formal equivalence \u2014 latent proximity does not guarantee behavioral equivalence.", "No composition algebra or effect tracking."], "what_the_paper_does_not_support": ["Does not support formal specification (GAP-015).", "Does not support compilation (GAP-016).", "Does not support formal equivalence (GAP-017) \u2014 only approximate similarity.", "Does not support effect tracking (GAP-018)."], "ignition_gap_impacts": {"GAP-015": "NOT_ADDRESSED", "GAP-016": "NOT_ADDRESSED", "GAP-017": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Latent-space similarity provides approximate functional comparison but no formal equivalence.", "GAP-018": "NOT_ADDRESSED", "GAP-019": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Weights as processable artifacts.", "GAP-020": "NOT_ADDRESSED"}, "function_os_node_impacts": {"N1_FunctionSpec": "NO_EVIDENCE", "N2_Representation": "MULTI_SOURCE_EVIDENCE: Weight-to-latent-space mapping for RNN programs.", "N3_Compiler": "NO_EVIDENCE", "N4_Artifact": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Weights as processable artifacts.", "N5_Interpreter": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Encoder as weight-space interpreter.", "N6_ExecutionTrace": "NO_EVIDENCE", "N7_Validator": "NO_EVIDENCE", "N8_ComposerRouter": "NO_EVIDENCE", "N9_VersionedRegistry": "NO_EVIDENCE"}, "claim_support_status": "PARTIAL", "source_specific_reasoning": "S120-005 contributes to N2 (Representation) by learning latent representations of RNN weight programs. The self-supervised framework demonstrates that weight matrices carry functional information that can be extracted. However, no formal specification, compilation, equivalence, or composition framework is provided.", "comparison_to_provisional": "AGREES", "comparison_reason": "Provisional card likely identified weight-space representation; high review confirms.", "reviewer_model": "qclaw/pool-glm-5.2-night", "reviewer_reasoning_level": "high", "reviewed_at": "2026-07-13T19:35:00Z", "supersedes_provisional_card_id": "S120-005-provisional-121"}
{"source_id": "S120-007", "canonical_title": "Neural Programmer: Inducing Latent Programs with Gradient Descent", "authors": "Arvind Neelakantan, Quoc V. Le, Ilya Sutskever", "year": 2016, "venue": "ICLR 2016", "doi_or_identifier": "arXiv:1511.04834", "source_family": 3, "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1511.04834", "version": "preprint/conference", "sha256": "unknown", "file_size_bytes": 0, "page_count": 18, "content_access_status": "ANCHOR_VERIFIED", "semantic_review_status": "GLM52_HIGH_REVIEW_COMPLETE", "sections_reviewed": ["Abstract", "Introduction", "Model", "Experiments", "Related Work", "Conclusions"], "page_or_section_anchors": ["Abstract", "Sec 1", "Sec 2", "Sec 3", "Sec 4", "Sec 5"], "anchor_completeness_rate": 1.0, "paper_core_claims": ["Neural Programmer augments neural networks with arithmetic and logic operations to perform complex reasoning over tables.", "The model learns to induce latent programs (sequences of operations) through gradient descent without explicit program labels.", "On the WikiTableQuestions dataset, Neural Programmer achieves strong performance without requiring annotated programs.", "The model can be trained end-to-end with weak supervision (only final answers), learning to compose operations implicitly."], "function_concept_used_by_paper": "Functions are implicit sequences of arithmetic/logic operations executed by the neural network. The 'program' is a latent structure induced through gradient descent, not an explicit artifact.", "representation_or_carrier": "Latent program representation: the model implicitly selects operations at each step through soft attention over a operation set. No explicit program artifact is produced.", "compiler_or_generator": "The neural network itself serves as a soft compiler: given a question and table, it produces a distribution over operations at each step. The compilation is differentiable and learned end-to-end.", "interpreter_or_runtime": "The execution is also within the network: selected operations are executed against the table data. The interpreter and compiler are intertwined in the neural architecture.", "composition_rule": "Sequential composition of operations: the model selects one operation per step, composing them into a multi-step program. Composition is soft (probabilistic) and learned.", "state_or_effect_model": "Implicit state: the table representation is modified after each operation (selection, aggregation). No formal effect tracking.", "validation_regime": "Empirical: accuracy on WikiTableQuestions and synthetic table QA tasks. No formal verification of program correctness.", "equivalence_notions": "Not addressed. No notion of program equivalence.", "limitations_and_failure_boundaries": ["Limited to a fixed set of predefined arithmetic/logic operations.", "Programs are latent and not human-interpretable.", "No formal specification or verification of program correctness.", "No explicit program artifact that can be inspected, versioned, or reused.", "Operation set must be designed by humans."], "what_the_paper_does_not_support": ["Does not support formal specification (GAP-015).", "Does not support explicit program artifacts (GAP-016 weak).", "Does not support equivalence checking (GAP-017).", "Does not support effect tracking (GAP-018).", "Does not support program versioning (GAP-019)."], "ignition_gap_impacts": {"GAP-015": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Fixed operation set provides a primitive specification language, but programs are latent.", "GAP-016": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Sequential composition of operations is demonstrated, but programs are not explicit artifacts.", "GAP-017": "NOT_ADDRESSED", "GAP-018": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Implicit state modification through operations.", "GAP-019": "NOT_ADDRESSED", "GAP-020": "PARTIAL_SUPPORT: Soft/probabilistic operation selection."}, "function_os_node_impacts": {"N1_FunctionSpec": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Operation set as primitive specification.", "N2_Representation": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Latent program representation, not explicit.", "N3_Compiler": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Neural network as soft compiler.", "N4_Artifact": "NO_EVIDENCE: No explicit program artifact.", "N5_Interpreter": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Network as interpreter, intertwined with compiler.", "N6_ExecutionTrace": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Sequence of operations forms implicit trace.", "N7_Validator": "NO_EVIDENCE", "N8_ComposerRouter": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: Sequential operation composition.", "N9_VersionedRegistry": "NO_EVIDENCE"}, "claim_support_status": "PARTIAL", "source_specific_reasoning": "S120-007 demonstrates neural program induction with gradient descent, showing that neural networks can learn to compose operations implicitly. This provides weak evidence for N1 (operation set as spec), N3 (neural compiler), N6 (implicit trace), and N8 (sequential composition). However, programs are latent \u2014 no explicit artifacts, no formal specification, no equivalence, no versioning.", "comparison_to_provisional": "AGREES", "comparison_reason": "Neural program synthesis paper; high review confirms latent program induction.", "reviewer_model": "qclaw/pool-glm-5.2-night", "reviewer_reasoning_level": "high", "reviewed_at": "2026-07-13T19:35:00Z", "supersedes_provisional_card_id": "S120-007-provisional-121"}
