 is a formal specification language for effects, though not for function value behavior.", "GAP-016": "NOT_ADDRESSED: No weight-space composition.", "GAP-017": "PARTIAL_SUPPORT: Equational equivalence (modulo theory equations) and observational equivalence (via handlers) are formal equivalence notions. But only for symbolic computations, not weight-space artifacts.", "GAP-018": "STRONG_SUPPORT: This is the foundational paper on algebraic effect handlers. It provides the definitive theoretical framework for effect tracking. Effects are first-class, formally specified, and composable. This is the strongest evidence for GAP-018.", "GAP-019": "NOT_ADDRESSED: No registry.", "GAP-020": "PARTIAL_SUPPORT: Probabilistic effects can be modeled as algebraic effects (probability distributions as operations). The paper mentions this possibility but does not develop it. Later work (S120-058, S120-064) makes this concrete."}, "function_os_node_impacts": {"N1_FunctionSpec": "PARTIAL_SUPPORT: Effect theories as formal specifications of computational effects. Operation signatures as effect contracts. This is a formal specification language for the effectful aspect of functions.", "N2_Representation": "NO_EVIDENCE: No weight-space representation. Pure symbolic/category-theoretic.", "N3_Compiler": "NO_EVIDENCE: Not applicable.", "N4_Artifact": "NO_EVIDENCE: No weight-space artifacts.", "N5_Interpreter": "MULTI_SOURCE_EVIDENCE: Handlers as interpreters. The universal property guarantees a unique interpretation. Multiple handlers can interpret the same computation differently. This is a concrete, formal interpreter abstraction.", "N6_ExecutionTrace": "MULTI_SOURCE_EVIDENCE: Computations are terms in the free model — essentially execution trees. Handlers traverse and interpret these trees. This is a formal execution trace with algebraic structure.", "N7_Validator": "WEAK_EVIDENCE: The logic for algebraic effects (Section 7) provides a verification framework. Equations serve as proof oblig