{"function_paradigm_id":"FP-121-001","paradigm_name":"Program-as-Weights (PAW)","source_family":1,"key_sources":["S120-001","S120-002","S120-004","S120-007"],"fulltext_backed":true,"definition":"A function is specified in natural language, compiled by a neural compiler into a small parameter-efficient adapter (weights), and executed by a frozen interpreter. The resulting artifact is reusable, locally executable, and cheaper than direct prompting of a large model.","supported_by_fulltext":"S120-001 demonstrates the PAW compiler/interpreter pair and FuzzyBench; S120-002 shows that linearised attention can be viewed as fast-weight programming; S120-004 surveys weight-space learning as a structured domain; S120-007 shows that neural networks can induce latent programs from weak supervision.","not_supported_by_fulltext":"No source provides a formal specification language, equivalence checker, or effect system for PAW functions. The results are empirical and domain-specific.","function_os_nodes":["1 FunctionSpec","2 Representation","3 Compiler","4 Artifact","5 Interpreter","6 ExecutionTrace","7 Validator","8 ComposerRouter","9 VersionedRegistry"],"node_relevance":{"1 FunctionSpec":"PARTIAL — natural language spec only, no formal contract","2 Representation":"STRONG — weights as function representation","3 Compiler":"STRONG — neural compiler trained end-to-end","4 Artifact":"STRONG — small adapter is the artifact","5 Interpreter":"STRONG — frozen interpreter executes weights","6 ExecutionTrace":"PARTIAL — execution is differentiable but not externally traceable","7 Validator":"PARTIAL — test-based validation only","8 ComposerRouter":"PARTIAL — no explicit composition shown","9 VersionedRegistry":"PARTIAL — no versioning shown"},"readjudication_summary":"FP-121-001 is a real and promising paradigm, but it is not a drop-in solution for all ignition function requirements. It lacks formal specification, equivalence checking, and effect tracking."}
{"function_paradigm_id":"FP-121-002","paradigm_name":"Hypernetwork-Generated Weights","source_family":2,"key_sources":["S120-009","S120-010","S120-011"],"fulltext_backed":true,"definition":"A hypernetwork (a smaller neural network) generates the weights of a main network conditioned on an embedding or task context. This decouples function specification from the final parameter tensor.","supported_by_fulltext":"S120-009 introduces hypernetworks for CNNs and LSTMs; S120-010 reviews hypernetworks across continual learning, federated learning, and transfer learning; S120-011 uses a central hypernetwork to generate personalized client models in federated learning.","not_supported_by_fulltext":"Hypernetworks do not provide a formal semantics for the generated function, nor do they solve equivalence checking or weight-space composition. They are typically trained end-to-end for a specific task family.","function_os_nodes":["1 FunctionSpec","2 Representation","3 Compiler","4 Artifact","8 ComposerRouter","9 VersionedRegistry"],"node_relevance":{"1 FunctionSpec":"PARTIAL — implicit in conditioning vector","2 Representation":"STRONG — generated weights as function representation","3 Compiler":"STRONG — hypernetwork as compiler","4 Artifact":"STRONG — generated weights are the artifact","5 Interpreter":"NOT_SHOWN — standard inference used","6 ExecutionTrace":"NOT_SHOWN","7 Validator":"NOT_SHOWN","8 ComposerRouter":"PARTIAL — conditional generation can be seen as routing","9 VersionedRegistry":"PARTIAL — context-conditioned models imply versioning"},"readjudication_summary":"FP-121-002 is well-established for weight generation and conditional adaptation. It does not, by itself, provide a full Function OS stack."}
{"function_paradigm_id":"FP-121-003","paradigm_name":"Execution-Guided Neural Program Synthesis","source_family":3,"key_sources":["S120-017","S120-018","S120-020","S120-021","S120-022"],"fulltext_backed":true,"definition":"A program is synthesized from a specification (natural language, I/O examples, pseudocode) and validated by execution. Execution signals guide search or generation.","supported_by_fulltext":"S120-017 uses latent execution for C program synthesis; S120-018 searches pseudocode-to-code with compilation and test feedback; S120-020 decomposes algorithms into hierarchical NL descriptions and searches over implementations; S120-021 guides generation with line-by-line execution signals; S120-022 extends reasoning via pseudo-program emulation and search.","not_supported_by_fulltext":"These systems are for code generation, not general function specification. They do not address weight-space artifacts, equivalence checking, or effect tracking.","function_os_nodes":["1 FunctionSpec","3 Compiler","5 Interpreter","6 ExecutionTrace","7 Validator","8 ComposerRouter"],"node_relevance":{"1 FunctionSpec":"PARTIAL — specs are natural language or I/O pairs","2 Representation":"NOT_SHOWN","3 Compiler":"STRONG — synthesis/search engine","4 Artifact":"PARTIAL — generated code","5 Interpreter":"STRONG — execution is central","6 ExecutionTrace":"STRONG — execution feedback is explicit","7 Validator":"STRONG — test-based validation","8 ComposerRouter":"PARTIAL — hierarchical decomposition in Parsel","9 VersionedRegistry":"NOT_SHOWN"},"readjudication_summary":"FP-121-003 is mature for code synthesis but remains focused on source-code artifacts. It provides a strong validation and execution model that can inform the Function OS, but not the weight-space representation layer."}
{"function_paradigm_id":"FP-121-004","paradigm_name":"Neural Operator Learning","source_family":4,"key_sources":["S120-027","S120-030"],"fulltext_backed":true,"definition":"Functions are learned as operators between Banach spaces of functions, typically for PDEs. A single neural operator represents an entire family of equations/parameters.","supported_by_fulltext":"S120-027 provides approximation-theoretic foundations for neural operators; S120-030 introduces the Fourier Neural Operator and demonstrates zero-shot super-resolution and speedups over classical solvers.","not_supported_by_fulltext":"Neural operators are specialized to PDE-like function-space maps. They do not provide a general-purpose function specification, composition algebra, or effect system.","function_os_nodes":["1 FunctionSpec","2 Representation","5 Interpreter","7 Validator"],"node_relevance":{"1 FunctionSpec":"PARTIAL — PDE parameter family is implicit","2 Representation":"STRONG — operator maps between function spaces","3 Compiler":"PARTIAL — training is the compilation process","4 Artifact":"STRONG — trained operator is the artifact","5 Interpreter":"STRONG — forward evaluation is interpretation","6 ExecutionTrace":"NOT_SHOWN","7 Validator":"PARTIAL — empirical error only","8 ComposerRouter":"NOT_SHOWN","9 VersionedRegistry":"NOT_SHOWN"},"readjudication_summary":"FP-121-004 is a rigorous mathematical paradigm for PDEs. It is not directly applicable to general computational functions, but its function-space view informs the representation layer."}
{"function_paradigm_id":"FP-121-005","paradigm_name":"Low-Rank Adaptation and Routing","source_family":5,"key_sources":["S120-031","S120-035","S120-036","S120-075"],"fulltext_backed":true,"definition":"Functions are represented as small low-rank adapter weights added to a frozen base model. Multiple adapters can be stored, retrieved, and composed for different tasks.","supported_by_fulltext":"S120-035 introduces LoRA; S120-031 routes and composes LoRA adapters via task representations; S120-036 composes adapters via vector-database retrieval and merging; S120-075 (MAML) prepares models for fast adaptation in weight space.","not_supported_by_fulltext":"Adapter composition is heuristic and empirically validated. There is no formal guarantee of compositional correctness, no equivalence checker, and no effect system.","function_os_nodes":["2 Representation","4 Artifact","8 ComposerRouter","9 VersionedRegistry"],"node_relevance":{"1 FunctionSpec":"PARTIAL — task is implicit, no formal contract","2 Representation":"STRONG — low-rank matrices as function representation","3 Compiler":"PARTIAL — fine-tuning is implicit compilation","4 Artifact":"STRONG — adapter is the artifact","5 Interpreter":"PARTIAL — standard forward pass","6 ExecutionTrace":"NOT_SHOWN","7 Validator":"PARTIAL — benchmark validation","8 ComposerRouter":"STRONG — routing/composition is the main focus","9 VersionedRegistry":"STRONG — adapter pools are versioned"},"readjudication_summary":"FP-121-005 is the closest practical paradigm to a versioned, composable function registry. It is strong on nodes 4, 8, and 9, but weak on formal specification and equivalence."}
{"function_paradigm_id":"FP-121-006","paradigm_name":"Weight-Space Averaging and Arithmetic","source_family":6,"key_sources":["S120-039","S120-045","S120-046"],"fulltext_backed":true,"definition":"Functions represented as weight checkpoints are combined by averaging, addition, negation, or sign-aware merging to produce a new model.","supported_by_fulltext":"S120-039 averages fine-tuned model weights (model soups); S120-045 defines task vectors and performs addition/negation/analogy in weight space; S120-046 resolves sign and redundancy interference during merging.","not_supported_by_fulltext":"These operations are empirical and depend on geometric assumptions (same low-error basin, similar architecture). They do not guarantee that the merged model implements a well-defined composition of the source functions.","function_os_nodes":["2 Representation","4 Artifact","8 ComposerRouter","9 VersionedRegistry"],"node_relevance":{"1 FunctionSpec":"NOT_SHOWN","2 Representation":"STRONG — weights as function representation","3 Compiler":"NOT_SHOWN","4 Artifact":"STRONG — merged model is the artifact","5 Interpreter":"PARTIAL — standard forward pass","6 ExecutionTrace":"NOT_SHOWN","7 Validator":"PARTIAL — empirical accuracy on benchmarks","8 ComposerRouter":"STRONG — arithmetic is composition","9 VersionedRegistry":"PARTIAL — requires multiple checkpoints"},"readjudication_summary":"FP-121-006 is a concrete instantiation of weight-space composition but lacks formal semantics. It should not be treated as strict function equivalence."}
{"function_paradigm_id":"FP-121-007","paradigm_name":"Refinement-Type Specifications","source_family":7,"key_sources":["S120-047","S120-050"],"fulltext_backed":true,"definition":"Function specifications are written as refinement types and verified by SMT. This provides a machine-checkable contract language.","supported_by_fulltext":"S120-047 introduces generic refinement types that abstract function contracts; S120-050 uses refinement reflection for complete SMT-based equational verification.","not_supported_by_fulltext":"These systems are for pure functional/Haskell/Rust-like programs, not for neural functions or weight-space artifacts. They do not address composition of neural artifacts or probabilistic semantics.","function_os_nodes":["1 FunctionSpec","2 Representation","7 Validator"],"node_relevance":{"1 FunctionSpec":"STRONG — refinement types are formal contracts","2 Representation":"PARTIAL — types describe functions, not weights","3 Compiler":"NOT_SHOWN","4 Artifact":"NOT_SHOWN","5 Interpreter":"NOT_SHOWN","6 ExecutionTrace":"NOT_SHOWN","7 Validator":"STRONG — SMT verification","8 ComposerRouter":"NOT_SHOWN","9 VersionedRegistry":"NOT_SHOWN"},"readjudication_summary":"FP-121-007 is essential for GAP-015 but applies to symbolic code, not neural functions. It must be integrated with other paradigms for a full Function OS."}
{"function_paradigm_id":"FP-121-008","paradigm_name":"Algebraic Effect Handlers","source_family":8,"key_sources":["S120-053","S120-055"],"fulltext_backed":true,"definition":"Functions carry effect signatures that are interpreted by handlers at composition/execution time, separating side effects from their implementation.","supported_by_fulltext":"S120-053 presents handlers for algebraic effects; S120-055 surveys operational semantics and implementations in Haskell, OCaml, SML, and Racket.","not_supported_by_fulltext":"Effect handlers are a language-level feature; they do not directly solve function specification, weight-space composition, or neural function equivalence.","function_os_nodes":["1 FunctionSpec","5 Interpreter","6 ExecutionTrace","8 ComposerRouter"],"node_relevance":{"1 FunctionSpec":"PARTIAL — effect signature is part of contract","2 Representation":"NOT_SHOWN","3 Compiler":"NOT_SHOWN","4 Artifact":"NOT_SHOWN","5 Interpreter":"STRONG — handlers define execution semantics","6 ExecutionTrace":"STRONG — effects are observable","7 Validator":"NOT_SHOWN","8 ComposerRouter":"PARTIAL — handlers can compose interpreters","9 VersionedRegistry":"NOT_SHOWN"},"readjudication_summary":"FP-121-008 is a strong candidate for GAP-018. It must be adapted to ignition's function representation layer."}
{"function_paradigm_id":"FP-121-009","paradigm_name":"Probabilistic Programming Semantics","source_family":9,"key_sources":["S120-058","S120-059","S120-064"],"fulltext_backed":true,"definition":"Functions are probabilistic programs with operational and denotational semantics, supported by inference and effect handlers.","supported_by_fulltext":"S120-058 introduces stochastic probabilities and nested inference; S120-059 gives operational/denotational semantics for higher-order probabilistic programs; S120-064 (Pyro) implements universal probabilistic programming with composable effect handlers.","not_supported_by_fulltext":"Probabilistic programming is a semantic framework, not a weight-space composition system. It does not address equivalence of neural artifacts or general function specifications.","function_os_nodes":["1 FunctionSpec","5 Interpreter","6 ExecutionTrace","7 Validator","8 ComposerRouter"],"node_relevance":{"1 FunctionSpec":"PARTIAL — probabilistic model is a specification","2 Representation":"NOT_SHOWN","3 Compiler":"NOT_SHOWN","4 Artifact":"NOT_SHOWN","5 Interpreter":"STRONG — inference engines interpret programs","6 ExecutionTrace":"STRONG — stochastic traces are observable","7 Validator":"PARTIAL — inference validation","8 ComposerRouter":"STRONG — effect handlers compose inference","9 VersionedRegistry":"PARTIAL — model components can be versioned"},"readjudication_summary":"FP-121-009 is the strongest candidate for GAP-020. It provides a rigorous probabilistic semantics that can be layered onto ignition's function model."}
{"function_paradigm_id":"FP-121-010","paradigm_name":"Versioned Reusable Skill Library","source_family":10,"key_sources":["S120-065","S120-070"],"fulltext_backed":true,"definition":"Functions are stored as executable skills in a library, discovered automatically, retrieved by need, and composed to solve new tasks.","supported_by_fulltext":"S120-065 (MUSE-Autoskill) creates, stores, and refines skills with a catalog; S120-070 (VOYAGER) maintains an ever-growing skill library and automatically retrieves/composes skills for Minecraft tasks.","not_supported_by_fulltext":"Skills are typically LLM-generated code snippets, not formally specified functions. There is no formal equivalence check or effect tracking beyond empirical execution.","function_os_nodes":["3 Compiler","5 Interpreter","6 ExecutionTrace","7 Validator","8 ComposerRouter","9 VersionedRegistry"],"node_relevance":{"1 FunctionSpec":"PARTIAL — natural language skill descriptions","2 Representation":"NOT_SHOWN","3 Compiler":"PARTIAL — LLM generates code","4 Artifact":"PARTIAL — skill code is the artifact","5 Interpreter":"STRONG — skills are executed","6 ExecutionTrace":"STRONG — execution traces are used","7 Validator":"STRONG — execution tests validate skills","8 ComposerRouter":"STRONG — retrieval and composition are central","9 VersionedRegistry":"STRONG — skill libraries are versioned"},"readjudication_summary":"FP-121-010 is a strong candidate for GAP-019. It demonstrates that a versioned, retrievable, composable function registry can be built and used in practice."}

